{
  "dom_id": "step-q236631",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Consider the following arithmetic sequence.\n\n$$\n-13,\\,-9,\\,-5,\\,-1,\\,…\n$$\n\nWhat is the $36$th term of this sequence?\n\n${a}_{36}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}+(n-1)d.\n$$\n\nFirst, we will write the formula for the $n$th term of this sequence. From the first few terms\n\n$$\n-13,\\,-9,\\,-5,\\,-1,\\,…,\n$$\n\nwe see that the first term is ${a}_{1}=-13$ and the common difference is \n\n$$\nd=-9-(-13)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute into the formula and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-13+(n-1)(4) \\\\  & =-13+4n-4 \\\\  & =4n-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use our formula to find the $36$th term. All we have to do is substitute $n=36:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =4n-17 \\\\ {a}_{36} & =4(36)-17 \\\\  & =144-17 \\\\  & =127\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
